  "brief": "The Fabric Bay wants one number for a cable loom and one for a hull with portholes, so the crew can tell shapes apart before anything is sewn.",
  "lesson": "A graph is a cell division with no faces, so its Euler characteristic is V − E; cutting a round hole out of a surface removes one face, so each hole lowers χ by 1.",
  "prompt": "In the Fabric Bay a cable loom is laid out as a grid of junctions in 3 rows and 5 columns. Each junction is joined by one straight cable to the junction next to it along its row and to the junction next to it down its column, and there are no other cables. Treat the loom as a graph: the junctions are its vertices, the cables are its edges, and it has no faces. Separately, a closed hull shaped like a torus (χ = 0) has 3 round portholes cut out of it, no two touching. Enter the number of edges E of the loom, then the Euler characteristic V − E of the loom, then the Euler characteristic of the hull with its portholes cut out.", "answer": [ 22, -7, -3 ], "labels": [ "edges E", "χ of the loom", "χ of the hull with portholes" ], "tolerance": 0.01,
  "hints": [ "In a grid, each row of junctions has one cable fewer than it has junctions, and so does each column. Add the cables along the rows to the cables down the columns.", "A graph has no faces, so its Euler characteristic is V − E. Cutting out a porthole removes one face and keeps every vertex and edge, so each porthole lowers χ by 1." ],
