  "teaching": { "intuition": "This review puts the chapter together. A panel can have some edges sewn in pairs and others left free. The sewn pairs work exactly as in a gluing word: tails go together, tips go together, and the corners fall into vertex classes. The free edges are sewn to nothing, so they form the rim of the panel. Each free edge runs between two vertex classes (or from a class back to itself), and if you follow free edges from point to point you always come back to where you started, so the free edges close up into boundary circles. A Möbius strip has one such circle and an annulus has two. For χ, count every edge after sewing: one for each letter and one for each free edge, with the panel as the single face. Finally, a disc sewn onto a boundary circle adds one face and nothing else, so capping every circle raises χ by the number of circles: the reverse of cutting holes.", "steps": [ "Number the corners and find the vertex classes from the sewn letters, as for a closed gluing word.", "For each free edge, write the two classes it joins, then follow free edges from class to class until you are back where you began: each closed run is one boundary circle.", "Count E as the number of letters plus the number of free edges. With F = 1, χ = V − E + 1.", "Capping each boundary circle with a disc adds one face, so the closed hull has χ plus the number of circles." ], "example": {
  "prompt": "A rectangle has the word a free a free (4 edges). How many boundary circles does it have, what is its χ, and what is χ once the circles are capped?", "steps": [ "Edge 1 (a) runs from corner 1 to corner 2 and edge 3 (a) from corner 3 to corner 4, both plain, so sewing joins corners 1 and 3 and corners 2 and 4: the classes are P = corners 1 and 3 and Q = corners 2 and 4.", "Edge 2 joins Q and P, and edge 4 joins Q and P. Out along one and back along the other is a single loop, so there is one boundary circle: this is the Möbius strip.", "E = 1 + 2 = 3 and F = 1, so χ = 2 − 3 + 1 = 0. Capping the one circle gives χ = 0 + 1 = 1, the projective plane." ], "answer": "1 boundary circle, χ = 0, and χ = 1 once it is capped." }, "pitfalls": [ "Counting each free edge as its own boundary circle. Free edges that meet at a vertex class are parts of the same circle.", "Leaving the free edges out of E. They are edges of the cell division too.", "Taking 1 away for each disc sewn on. A disc adds a face, so capping raises χ." ] },
